---
/* THE PAGE THAT SAYS HOW TO PLUG INTO THE ATLAS, FOR SOMEBODY WHO HAS NEVER DONE IT.
 *
 * /data/ lists the files and their licences, which is the right page for somebody who already knows what a JSON file is. This is
 * the other half: four things a researcher wants to DO (read the sources for a place, keep their own progress, use every spelling
 * of a name, ask who governed a place in a year), each as a short recipe with a "try it" that calls the real file or service from
 * this page, so what is on the screen is what the interface answers today, not a description of it.
 *
 * AND IT SAYS WHAT DOES NOT EXIST. The last section is a list of gaps found while writing it (no CORS on the data files, no
 * place-name lookup over the API, no way to add a single mark...). A page that only described what works would send somebody to build
 * on a door that is painted on. Every claim here was checked against the live services on 12 Nov 2026 or against the code that serves
 * them; nothing is promised that is not shipped.
 *
 * Counts are read from the shipped files at build time, so they cannot drift.
 */
import fs from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";
import provs from "../../public/providers.json";
import ctx from "../../public/context.json";
import gn from "../../public/given-names.json";
import sf from "../../public/surname-families.json";
import idx from "../../public/index.json";

const PUB = fs.existsSync(path.join(process.cwd(), "public")) ? path.join(process.cwd(), "public") : path.join(process.cwd(), "site", "public");
const nPlaceFiles = (() => { try { return fs.readdirSync(path.join(PUB, "p")).filter((f) => f.endsWith(".json")).length; } catch { return 0; } })();
const N = (n) => Number(n).toLocaleString("en-GB");
const cnt = ctx.counts || {};
const api = "https://api.recordatlas.org";
const syncHost = "https://sync.recordatlas.org";
const aiHost = "https://ai.recordatlas.org";
const ver = (ctx.contract && ctx.contract.version) || "";
---
<Base
  title="Connect to the Atlas"
  eyebrow="Read it · write your research to it · from a spreadsheet, a script, or an AI assistant"
  dek="Four things you can do with the Atlas's data without opening the website, each as a short recipe you can try on this page. And the things that do not work yet, listed honestly.">

  <div class="k-stats" id="cx-status" aria-live="polite">
    <span class="k-tag">Read API <b id="st-api">checking…</b></span>
    <span class="k-tag">Sync <b id="st-sync">checking…</b></span>
    <span class="k-tag">Ask&nbsp;Claude <b id="st-ai">checking…</b></span>
    <span><b>{N(nPlaceFiles)}</b> place files</span>
    <span><b>{N(cnt.regionEras || 0)}</b> jurisdiction eras</span>
    <span><b>{N(gn.totalForms || 0)}</b> given-name forms</span>
  </div>

  <div class="k-hi">
    <p><b>You do not need to be a programmer to start.</b> The Atlas keeps everything in plain files with web addresses. A web address
    you can paste into a browser is already an API. The harder doors (saving your own research) are explained step by step below, and the
    page will show you exactly what your computer would send, without sending anything.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="k-h" id="doors">Pick your door <span class="k-count">what you want to do</span></h2>
  <ul class="k-tiles cx-doors">
    <li><a href="#places"><b>1 · What sources can I read for a place?</b></a><span>Archives, registers and year-ranges for one village. Read-only, no login.</span></li>
    <li><a href="#research"><b>2 · Keep my own research progress</b></a><span>Write "I searched this book, found it" from your own tool. Needs the six words.</span></li>
    <li><a href="#names"><b>3 · Use every spelling of a name</b></a><span>Ivan is Johann is Giovanni. Ask once, search them all.</span></li>
    <li><a href="#eras"><b>4 · Who governed this place in 1850?</b></a><span>Places, countries, flags and their eras, with the years, the polity and the archive that filed them.</span></li>
  </ul>

