  <!-- 1 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="k-h" id="places">1 · What sources can I read for a place? <span class="k-count">read-only, no key</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">Every place the Atlas knows has one small file at <code>/p/&lt;place-id&gt;.json</code>. It lists the <b>collections</b> (which archive or site holds
    records for it, what years, what it costs to look) and the <b>volumes</b> (individual books, with their FamilySearch waypoint). The
    place page you see in the Atlas is drawn from exactly this file.</p>
  <ol class="k-steps">
    <li><b>Find the place id.</b> It is the web address of its page: <code>recordatlas.org/place/<mark>senj</mark>/</code> means the id is <code>senj</code>. (There is no search-by-name API yet, see the gaps below.)</li>
    <li><b>Fetch the file.</b> <code>GET https://recordatlas.org/p/senj.json</code> Anyone, no key.</li>
    <li><b>Read <code>collections</code>.</b> Each row is one archive or site: <code>provider</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>from</code>/<code>to</code> years, <code>access</code> (free · account · paid · onsite…), <code>url</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="cx-try" id="try-places">
    <div class="cx-bar"><b>Try it</b>
      <label>place id <input id="pl-id" value="senj" size="16" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off"></label>
      <button type="button" data-p="senj">Senj</button><button type="button" data-p="ovaro">Ovaro</button><button type="button" data-p="rijeka">Rijeka</button>
      <button type="button" class="go" id="pl-go">Fetch it</button></div>
    <div class="cx-out" id="pl-out" aria-live="polite"><p class="cx-hint">Press <b>Fetch it</b>: the page will call <code>/p/senj.json</code> and draw what comes back.</p></div>
    <details class="cx-code"><summary>The same thing in code</summary>
<pre set:text={`curl -s https://recordatlas.org/p/senj.json | jq '.collections[] | {provider, title, from, to, access}'

// JavaScript (run it on a server or in a script; see the gaps for why a web page on another site cannot)
const place = await (await fetch("https://recordatlas.org/p/senj.json")).json();
for (const c of place.collections) console.log(c.provider, c.title, c.from + "–" + c.to, c.access);`}></pre></details>
  </div>
  <p class="cx-see"><b>How it looks in the Atlas:</b> the same rows are the "Where the records are" list on <a href={u("/place/senj/")}>the Senj page</a>; <code>access</code> sets the dot's colour (see <a href={u("/help/")}>Help</a>).
    For a whole country at once: <code>{api}/v1/providers?country=HR</code> (the archives and sites, with how each charges).</p>

  <!-- 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="k-h" id="research">2 · Keep my own research progress <span class="k-count">write · six words</span></h2>
  <div class="k-hi is-plain"><p><b>There is no account. Your six words are the account.</b></p>
    <p>Six words from a public list of 1,024 words (60 bits: strong against guessing, not against being told, so keep them like a house key).
    Your browser, or your own tool, turns them into four secrets. The server only ever sees a folder name, two passwords and a sealed box it cannot open.</p></div>

  <figure class="cx-fig">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 880 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="six-t six-d">
      <title id="six-t">What the six words become</title>
      <desc id="six-d">Six words are stretched by PBKDF2 into a master secret, which HKDF splits into a lookup id, a read token, a write token and an encryption key. The key stays with you. The id and tokens go to the server with the sealed copy.</desc>
      <defs><marker id="ah2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" fill="var(--ink-3)"/></marker></defs>
      <rect x="10" y="100" width="200" height="100" rx="14" class="svg-box"/>
      <text x="110" y="135" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-h">six words</text>
      <text x="110" y="160" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-mono">anchor mimic …</text>
      <text x="110" y="184" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s2">you keep these</text>
      <path d="M210 150 H262" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/>
      <text x="236" y="140" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s2">PBKDF2</text><text x="236" y="170" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s2">600k×</text>
      <rect x="266" y="120" width="110" height="60" rx="12" class="svg-box"/>
      <text x="321" y="147" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-h2">master</text><text x="321" y="166" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s2">never stored</text>
      <path d="M376 150 H410" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/>
      <text x="393" y="140" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s2">HKDF</text>
      <g>
        <rect x="414" y="14" width="236" height="56" rx="10" class="svg-read"/><text x="428" y="38" class="svg-h2">id</text><text x="428" y="57" class="svg-s2">32 hex · the folder name on the server</text>
        <rect x="414" y="80" width="236" height="56" rx="10" class="svg-read"/><text x="428" y="104" class="svg-h2">read token</text><text x="428" y="123" class="svg-s2">proves you may fetch the copy</text>
        <rect x="414" y="146" width="236" height="56" rx="10" class="svg-write"/><text x="428" y="170" class="svg-h2">write token</text><text x="428" y="189" class="svg-s2">proves you may replace or delete it</text>
        <rect x="414" y="212" width="236" height="56" rx="10" class="svg-key"/><text x="428" y="236" class="svg-h2">AES-256 key</text><text x="428" y="255" class="svg-s2">seals your research · stays with you</text>
      </g>
      <g stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none" marker-end="url(#ah2)">
        <path d="M410 150 C 400 100, 400 60, 410 44"/><path d="M410 150 C 405 120, 405 110, 410 108"/><path d="M410 152 C 405 160, 405 168, 410 174"/><path d="M410 154 C 400 200, 400 230, 410 240"/>
      </g>
      <rect x="690" y="40" width="180" height="150" rx="14" class="svg-box"/>
      <text x="780" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-h">Server holds</text>
      <text x="780" y="94" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s">id · token hashes</text>
      <text x="780" y="114" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s">one sealed box</text>
      <text x="780" y="134" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s">(30 daily copies)</text>
      <text x="780" y="168" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s2">cannot open the box</text>
      <path d="M650 100 H686" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/><path d="M650 160 H686" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/>
      <rect x="690" y="212" width="180" height="56" rx="12" class="svg-key"/><text x="780" y="236" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-h2">sealed box</text><text x="780" y="255" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-s2">AES-GCM, bound to your id</text>
      <path d="M650 240 H686" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 4" marker-end="url(#ah2)"/>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>The same six words on a phone, a laptop or a script give the same four values, so any of them can fetch and decrypt your copy.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="cx-twocol">
    <div class="cx-card"><h3>Who holds which secret</h3>
      <table class="cx-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Goes to server?</th><th>What it lets someone do</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>yes, in the web address</td><td>nothing alone: it only names the folder</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>read token</code></td><td>yes, as <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code> on a GET</td><td>download the sealed box (still sealed)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>write token</code></td><td>yes, on PUT and DELETE</td><td>replace or erase the box. First save also sends the read token so the server can store its hash.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>AES key</code></td><td><b>never</b></td><td>open the box. Only your six words make it.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p class="cx-note">The server stores <i>hashes</i> of the tokens and compares them. A wrong token and an unknown id both answer 404, so it will not even say whether an id exists.</p></div>
    <div class="cx-card"><h3>What goes in the box</h3>
      <p>The same research file the website saves: <code>kind: "record-atlas-research"</code>, version 2. One entry per <b>volume</b> you have marked, under the <b>line</b> (family) it belongs to.</p>
<pre class="cx-pre" id="pack-example" set:text={`{
  "v": 2, "kind": "record-atlas-research", "exported": "2026-11-12",
  "lines": { "kosina-senj": { "name": "Kosina", "where": "Senj", "made": "2026-11-12" } },
  "active": "kosina-senj",
  "marks": {
    "volume:9RK1-T3F": {
      "t": "Births (Rođeni) 1734-1756", "where": "senj", "href": "/place/senj/", "total": 174,
      "walked": [],
      "by": { "kosina-senj": { "done": [[1, 47]], "state": "found", "since": "2026-11-12" } }
    }
  }
}`}></pre>
      <p class="cx-note"><code>state</code> is one of <code>planned · written · searched · found · exhausted · blocked</code>. <code>walked</code> is only for a book you read <i>every entry</i> of; it then counts for every family line, so never set it for "I looked for my surname".</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="cx-h3">Try the keys: nothing is sent anywhere</h3>
  <div class="cx-try" id="try-keys">
    <div class="cx-bar"><b>Throwaway demo</b>
      <button type="button" class="go" id="kd-make">Make six words and show what they become</button>
      <span class="cx-hint" id="kd-busy" hidden>working: 600,000 rounds of PBKDF2…</span></div>
    <div class="cx-out" id="kd-out" aria-live="polite"><p class="cx-hint">This runs the real key code from the Atlas in your browser. It makes a <b>throwaway</b> phrase (do not use it for real research), shows the values, then builds the exact save request <b>without sending it</b>.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="cx-h3">The save, step by step (any language)</h3>
  <ol class="k-steps">
    <li><b>Words → keys.</b> <code>PBKDF2-SHA256(phrase, salt "record-atlas-sync-v1", 600000 rounds) → 32 bytes</code>, then <code>HKDF-SHA256</code> with <code>info</code> = <code>record-atlas-sync/id</code> (16 bytes → hex), <code>/read</code>, <code>/write</code> (32 bytes → base64url) and <code>/enc</code> (the AES key). Words are lower-case, single spaces.</li>
    <li><b>Seal it.</b> <code>AES-256-GCM</code>, a fresh 12-byte IV, the <b>id</b> as associated data. The box is <code>[1][iv][ciphertext+tag]</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Save it.</b> <code>PUT {syncHost}/v1/&lt;id&gt;</code> with <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;write token&gt;</code>, and on the <b>first</b> save also <code>X-Read-Token: &lt;read token&gt;</code>. Body = the box. Answer: <code>{`{"ok":true,"saved":"…","bytes":1234}`}</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Get it back.</b> <code>GET /v1/&lt;id&gt;</code> with the read token; <code>/versions</code> lists the last 30 daily copies. <code>DELETE /v1/&lt;id&gt;</code> with the write token erases everything, in both buckets.</li>
  </ol>
  <details class="cx-code" open><summary>A working example (Node 20 or a browser console)</summary>
<pre set:text={`// Node 20+. Download the Atlas's own file once, so the format cannot drift:
//   curl -O https://recordatlas.org/js/research-sync.js
const SY = require("./research-sync.js");                 // same code the website runs (also loads in a browser as window.raResearchSync)
const words = (await (await fetch("https://recordatlas.org/sync-words.json")).json()).words;

const phrase = SY.makePassphrase(words);                  // six words in one string. SAVE THEM: nobody can recover them
const keys   = await SY.deriveKeys(phrase);               // { id, readToken, writeToken, key }
const pack   = JSON.stringify({ v: 2, kind: "record-atlas-research", exported: "2026-11-12",
  lines: { "kosina-senj": { name: "Kosina", where: "Senj", made: "2026-11-12" } }, active: "kosina-senj",
  marks: { "volume:9RK1-T3F": { t: "Births 1734-1756", where: "senj", href: "/place/senj/", total: 174, walked: [],
           by: { "kosina-senj": { done: [[1, 47]], state: "found", since: "2026-11-12" } } } } });
await SY.push(keys, pack);                                // PUT, sealed. Returns { ok, saved, bytes }
const back = await SY.pull(keys);                         // GET, opened. back.text is your JSON; null if nothing is stored
// later, on any machine: SY.deriveKeys(SY.normalisePassphrase(typedWords, words).phrase)
`}></pre></details>

  <p class="cx-see"><b>How it looks in the Atlas:</b> open <a href={u("/backup/")}>Connection &amp; Backup</a>, choose <i>Restore from my passphrase</i> and type the six words: the marks come back onto
    <a href={u("/my-research/")}>My research</a> and onto every volume row on the place pages, as the little state chips below.</p>
  <div class="cx-chips" aria-label="The six states a volume can be in">
    <span class="chip s-planned">planning to</span><span class="chip s-written">written, no reply</span><span class="chip s-searched">searched, nothing found</span>
    <span class="chip s-found">found something</span><span class="chip s-exhausted">read everything here</span><span class="chip s-blocked">cannot get access</span>
  </div>

  <!-- 3 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="k-h" id="names">3 · Use every spelling of a name <span class="k-count">read · live API</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">A register is indexed under the clerk's form, not yours. <b>{N(gn.rows.length)}</b> given names carry <b>{N(gn.totalForms)}</b> forms in <b>{gn.languages.length}</b> languages
    ({N(gn.attested)} met on a real record), and <b>{N(sf.rows.length)}</b> surname families list their spellings. Each form says whether it was <i>seen</i> on a record, is only a <i>lead</i>
    (the right form in that language, never yet met) or a <i>rule</i> (made by grammar, like the Latin cases). A lead is something to search, never a finding.</p>
  <div class="cx-try" id="try-names">
    <div class="cx-bar"><b>Try it</b>
      <label>given name <input id="nm-in" value="Ivan" size="14" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off"></label>
      <button type="button" data-n="Ivan">Ivan</button><button type="button" data-n="Joannes">Joannes</button><button type="button" data-n="Zuane">Zuane</button>
      <button type="button" class="go" id="nm-go">Look it up</button></div>
    <div class="cx-out" id="nm-out" aria-live="polite"><p class="cx-hint">Press <b>Look it up</b>: the page asks <code>{api}/v1/given-name/Ivan</code>.</p></div>
    <details class="cx-code"><summary>The same thing in code</summary>
<pre set:text={`curl -s ${api}/v1/given-name/Ivan
curl -s "${api}/v1/term?q=uxoris&record=baptism"       # what a Latin register word means
curl -s -G ${api}/v1/date --data-urlencode "text=die 30 7bris 1671"  # 30 September 1671 (7bris = September)
# surname spellings are a file, not yet an endpoint:
curl -s https://recordatlas.org/surname-families.json | jq '.rows[] | select(.name=="Defranceschi")'`}></pre></details>
  </div>
  <p class="cx-see"><b>How it looks in the Atlas:</b> <a href={u("/your-name/")}>Say your name</a> shows the same families, and every surname page has a "spellings" panel. <b>Writing</b> a new spelling is not an API: it goes
    through a reviewed catalogue (<a href={u("/add/")}>send it with the evidence</a>).</p>

  <!-- 4 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="k-h" id="eras">4 · Places, countries, flags and their eras <span class="k-count">read · files</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">The jurisdiction layer has <b>{N(cnt.regions || regions.regions.length)}</b> regions with <b>{N(cnt.regionEras || 0)}</b> eras, and <b>{N(cnt.countries || 0)}</b> countries with <b>{N(cnt.countryEras || 0)}</b> eras.
    Each era says <b>from</b> and <b>to</b> (a year equal to a boundary belongs to the <i>newer</i> era), the <b>regime</b> (a short id such as <code>habsburg</code>, labelled in <code>regimes</code>), the <b>polity</b>, a <b>note</b>, and <b>filedUnder</b>: which provider holds the records for those years.
    The contract is versioned (now <code>{ver}</code>) and described in <a href={u("/data/#context")}>Use the data</a>. Places without a region, and {cnt.gaps} gaps and {cnt.overlaps} overlaps in the table, are listed in <code>context.json</code> as <code>quality</code>: stay silent there.</p>
  <ol class="k-steps">
    <li><b>Place → region.</b> A place file (<code>/p/senj.json</code>) names its <code>region</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Region → eras.</b> <code>/regions.json</code> (2.7 MB; <code>.regions[]</code>) has <code>eras[]</code> for each. Pick the one with <code>from ≤ year &lt; to</code> (<code>to: null</code> means "still").</li>
    <li><b>Era → archive.</b> <code>filedUnder</code> is a list of provider ids; look them up in <code>/providers.json</code> to see what each costs.</li>
    <li><b>Flags.</b> <code>/flag-index.json</code> ({N(cnt.countries || 0)} countries), <code>/era-flags.json</code> (inline art per era), and <code>/flags/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> pages with the history of each flag.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="cx-try" id="try-eras">
    <div class="cx-bar"><b>Try it</b>
      <label>place id <input id="er-id" value="senj" size="14" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off"></label>
      <label>year <input id="er-year" type="number" value="1858" min="800" max="2026" style="width:5.5em"></label>
      <button type="button" class="go" id="er-go">Who governed it?</button>
      <span class="cx-hint" id="er-busy" hidden>fetching regions.json (2.7 MB)…</span></div>
    <div class="cx-out" id="er-out" aria-live="polite"><p class="cx-hint">Press the button: the page reads the place, its region, and picks the era for that year.</p></div>
    <details class="cx-code"><summary>The same thing in code</summary>
<pre set:text={`const place   = await (await fetch("https://recordatlas.org/p/senj.json")).json();
const regions = (await (await fetch("https://recordatlas.org/regions.json")).json()).regions;
const era = regions.find(r => r.id === place.region).eras
              .find(e => e.from <= 1858 && (e.to === null || 1858 < e.to));
console.log(era.regime, era.polity, era.filedUnder);`}></pre></details>
  </div>
  <p class="cx-see"><b>How it looks in the Atlas:</b> the same rows are the "who governed it" table on a <a href={u("/region/kvarner/")}>region page</a>, the colour bands when you set a year on the map, and the flag beside each era on a country's <a href={u("/flags/")}>flag page</a>.</p>

  <!-- A researcher's day ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="k-h">A researcher's day, using only these doors <span class="k-count">an example</span></h2>
  <ol class="cx-day">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><div><b>Where do I look?</b> <code>/p/senj.json</code> lists ten collections for Senj: FamilySearch, an archive, an index…</div></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><div><b>Whose records were they in 1858?</b> Regions → <code>kvarner</code> → the 1815–1918 era is Habsburg; <code>filedUnder</code> names the archives that hold it.</div></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><div><b>Which spellings do I search?</b> <code>/v1/given-name/…</code> for the forenames, <code>surname-families.json</code> for the surname.</div></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><div><b>Write down what I did.</b> One <code>PUT</code> of the sealed research file keyed by the six words, saying "Births 1734–1756, pages 1–47: found".</div></li>
    <li><span class="n">5</span><div><b>Next day, on the phone.</b> Same six words → same id → <code>GET</code> → decrypt. The marks are back on the map.</div></li>
  </ol>

  <!-- gaps ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="k-h" id="gaps">What does not work yet <span class="k-count">found while writing this page</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">These are real, checked against the live services on 12 November 2026. They are listed in the order a new builder would hit them.</p>
  <div class="scroll cx-gaps"><table class="k-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Gap</th><th>What happens</th><th>What would fix it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Other websites cannot call most of it from a browser</b></td><td>The data files (<code>recordatlas.org/*.json</code>) send no <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> header, and Sync and Ask&nbsp;Claude answer browser requests only from <code>recordatlas.org</code> itself (tested: a page on another origin is refused). Only the read API allows any origin. Scripts, spreadsheets and servers are unaffected, because they send no origin.</td><td>One header in <code>workers/pages/src/index.js</code> for the files; for Sync a deliberate choice (the allow-list is also an abuse guard), perhaps an opt-in list of registered apps.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>No place lookup by name</b></td><td>Over the API you cannot ask "which id is Senj?". <code>/index.json</code> lists the {N(idx.places.length)} busiest places with their ids; the rest sit in {N(Object.keys((idx.tail && idx.tail.cells) || {}).length)} grid files (<code>/pi/&lt;lat&gt;_&lt;lon&gt;.json</code>, 5-degree squares), so you must know roughly where a place is. The <code>/a/</code> name files give name, coordinates and country but <b>no id</b>.</td><td>The planned <code>atlas_find_place</code>, or an id column in the name files.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>The API only has five tools</b></td><td>Given names, register terms, dates, providers by country, and the data contract. Nothing per place, per volume, per surname or per era.</td><td><code>atlas_place</code>, <code>atlas_volumes</code>, <code>atlas_eras</code>, <code>atlas_surname</code> (designed in <code>docs/atlas-mcp-plan.md</code>, not built).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>You can only write your whole research, not one mark</b></td><td>Sync replaces the whole sealed copy; the newest save wins. Two tools saving at once can overwrite each other, and there is no "add this one mark" call.</td><td>A merge-on-write endpoint, or a documented read-merge-write recipe with a version check.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Writing needs the encryption done by you</b></td><td>No official library for Python or Node and no test vectors, so a third party must re-implement PBKDF2, HKDF and AES-GCM exactly. The browser file <code>research-sync.js</code> is the only reference.</td><td>A small published package and a page of known-answer test values.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>The six words open both read and write</b></td><td>There is no read-only phrase to hand a relative or a society. Whoever has the words can also overwrite or erase.</td><td>A separate "share read-only" code derived from the same words.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>No schema for the research file</b></td><td>The shape above is read off the code. There is no JSON Schema, so a wrong <code>state</code> or range is not refused until the website tries to open it.</td><td>A published schema, checked on save.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Only your own copy can be written</b></td><td>There is no API for adding a provider, a place alias or a spelling to the shared catalogue. Those are proposals reviewed by a person.</td><td>Propose-first write tools, which are planned, not built.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>No keys, quotas or limits are documented</b></td><td>The read API has optional per-app keys made by hand; limits are dashboard rules. The data page says "no rate limit", which is true of the files, not the Workers.</td><td>One page of limits per service, and a way to ask for a key.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Sync has hardly been used</b></td><td>The live counter at <code>{syncHost}/v1/count</code> reports <b id="gap-count">…</b> registrations. It works in tests; it has little real mileage.</td><td>Ask three researchers to use it for a month and report.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>A README still says "not deployed"</b></td><td><code>workers/api/README.md</code> and <code>workers/sync/README.md</code> say not deployed; both answer on the live hosts.</td><td>Update the READMEs.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>

  <!-- reference ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="k-h" id="reference">Quick reference <span class="k-count">every address in one table</span></h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table class="cx-tbl cx-ref">
    <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Method</th><th>Needs</th><th>Gives you</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>recordatlas.org/p/&lt;id&gt;.json</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>one place: collections, volumes, region, parishes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>recordatlas.org/regions.json</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>{N(regions.regions.length)} regions and their eras</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>recordatlas.org/context.json</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>the era contract, counts, quality gaps (v{ver})</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>recordatlas.org/providers.json</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>{N(provs.providers.length)} archives and sites, with access class</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>recordatlas.org/given-names.json</code> · <code>surname-families.json</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>every form of a name (CC0)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>recordatlas.org/flag-index.json</code> · <code>era-flags.json</code> · <code>flag-eras.json</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>flags by country and era</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>recordatlas.org/research-sessions.json</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>what the family archives have published</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{api}/v1/given-name/&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>forms of a given name by language</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{api}/v1/term?q=&amp;record=</code> · <code>/v1/date?text=</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>register words · date reading</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{api}/v1/providers?country=XX</code> · <code>/v1/context</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>archives for a country · data build info</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{api}/mcp</code></td><td>POST</td><td>nothing</td><td>the same tools for AI assistants (see below)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{syncHost}/v1/&lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>PUT · GET · DELETE</td><td>six words (tokens)</td><td>save · fetch · erase your sealed research</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{syncHost}/v1/&lt;id&gt;/versions[/&lt;day&gt;]</code></td><td>GET</td><td>read token</td><td>the last 30 daily copies</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{syncHost}/v1/count</code></td><td>GET</td><td>nothing</td><td>how many people keep a copy</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{aiHost}/v1/translate · transcribe · document · casefile</code></td><td>POST</td><td>Turnstile check, optional six-word id</td><td>Claude reads a pasted line or photo; a few free tries a day</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>{aiHost}/v1/allowance</code></td><td>GET</td><td>optional id</td><td>tries left today</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>

  <h2 class="k-h" id="ai">Using it from an AI assistant</h2>
  <ol class="k-steps">
    <li><b>Claude:</b> Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector → paste <code>{api}/mcp</code>. No login. You can then ask "what does <i>uxoris</i> mean in a baptism?" or "what is the Latin for Ivan?" and it calls the Atlas.</li>
    <li><b>Any other assistant that speaks MCP:</b> point it at the same address (streamable HTTP, JSON replies).</li>
    <li><b>What it can and cannot do:</b> it reads the five tools above and says which file each answer came from. An empty answer means "not in the catalogue", never "does not exist". It cannot save your research (that needs the six words, which an assistant should never be given).</li>
  </ol>

